          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %strong compactness theorem
            is a fundamental result of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+theory"} model theory
            on the existence of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/models"} models
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+theories"} first-order theories
          %p
            Historically it goes back to work of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G%C3%B6del"} Gödel
            and Mal’cev in the 1930s.
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lindstr%C3%B6m%27s+theorem"} Lindström's theorem
            uses the compactness and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L%C3%B6wenheim-Skolem+theorem"} Löwenheim-Skolem theorem
            to characterize classical first-order logic. The validity or non-validity of the compactness theorem in logical systems other than first-order logic plays under the name of
            %em compactness property
            an important role in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abstract+model+theory"} abstract model theory
          %h2#theorem Theorem
          %p
            Briefly, the compactness theorem states that a set of first-order formulas
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Φ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi
            has a model precisely iff every finite subset of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Φ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi
            has a model.
          %h3#remark Remark
          %p
            There are different proofs for the compactness theorem e.g. an entirely ‘semantic’ one using
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ultraproduct"} ultraproducts
            = succeed ":" do
              %sup#fnref:1
                %a{:href => "#fn:1", :rel => "footnote"} 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Φ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi
            has no model, due to completeness this implies that there is a contradiction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi φ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Φ
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi φ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi\vdash\varphi
            but such a deduction of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi φ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi
            can involve only a finite subset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Φ
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi Φ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi_0\subset \Phi
            hence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Φ
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi φ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi_0\vdash\varphi
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Φ
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi_0
            would have no model as well.
          %p Despite the intuitive appeal, this approach obliges one to establish the completeness theorem, which depends on fiddly details of formal proofs and deductive systems to make rigorous.
          %h3#compactness_for_propositional_logic Compactness for propositional logic
          %p The motivation for the terminology stems from the observation that the compactness theorem literally expresses the compactness of a suitable topology on first-order structures.
          %h2#illustrations Illustrations
          %p As the compactness theorem is arguably the most fundamental result of model theory, there are too many examples of its use for us to do it much justice here. But perhaps a few examples here will help illustrate some typical uses.
          %h3#totalorder Total orders
          #total.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              Every set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              can be totally ordered.
          %p
            Of course this trivially follows from the stronger result that every set can be
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-ordered+set"} well-ordered
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ultrafilter+theorem"} ultrafilter theorem
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              Introduce a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/language"} language
              (i.e.,
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/signature+%28in+logic%29"} signature
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_p
              for each
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \in X
              and a binary relation symbol
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory"} theory
              take axioms
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mi p
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg (c_p = c_q)
              whenever
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo ≠
                    %mi q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \neq q
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              and the usual axioms to make
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/total+order"} total order
              (reflexivity, transitivity, antisymmetry, and connectedness). This theory is finitely satisfiable: any finite subset
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              of the axioms has a model, by interpreting each
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_x
              occurring in an inequality axiom belonging to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              simply as
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              in some way to interpret
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_x
              can be interpreted as the bottom element of that total order without any harm. By the compactness theorem, this theory has a model
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              to the set of all constants
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_x
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              is used to totally order all the
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
          %p
            With slightly more effort, a similar argument can be given to show that every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+order"} partial order
            can be extended to a total order. We just proved the case where the partial order is discrete.
          %h4#choicelike_consequences Choice-like consequences
          %p
            Again, we assume the ultrafilter principle but do not assume the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
          #surj.num_cor
            %h6#corollary Corollary
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo :
                    %mi F
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p: F \to X
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surjection"} surjective
              and every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber"} fiber
              is finite, then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              has a section
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              Use Proposition
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#total"}
              to totally order
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-ordered+set"} well-order
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s(x)
              for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
              to be the least element in the fiber
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_x
              (which is nonempty because
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              is surjective).
          #countable.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p A countable disjoint union of finite sets is again countable.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_3 Proof
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_n
              be the countable family and let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              be the union; again we have a fibering
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \to \mathbb{N}
              with fiber
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_n
              over
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#total"}
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_n
              inherits an order from
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lexicographic+order"} lexicographic order
              on the set of pairs
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo ′
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F' = \{(n, x): n \in \mathbb{N}, x \in F_n\}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo ′
                    %mo →
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F' \to F
              is a bijection, so we obtain a well-ordering of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              by transport of structure (differing, probably, from the original total ordering of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              which has done its job but is of no further use). Then we can define a partial map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo :
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo →
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi: \mathbb{N} \to F
              by induction:
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(0)
              is the bottom of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(n+1)
              is (if it exists) the bottom of the complement of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi([0, n])
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
              onto
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
          #wkl.num_cor
            %h6#corollary_2 Corollary
            %p
              %strong (Weak König’s lemma)
              Every infinite
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tree"} tree
              for which there is finite branching at each node has an infinite branch.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_4 Proof
            %p
              A tree is by definition the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+elements"} category of elements
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e = (n, x \in F(n))
              of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F: \mathbb{N}^{op} \to Set
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
              has its usual order and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(0)
              is terminal. A
              %em descendant
              of an element
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e = (n, x \in F(n))
              is an element that maps to
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi e
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi m
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi m
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (m, y \in F(m))
              with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                    %mo ≥
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m \geq n
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi m
                      %mo ≥
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(m \geq n)(y) = x
              %em child
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
              is such a descendant with
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi m
                      %mo =
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m = n+1
            %p
              By the branching hypothesis, the set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_n
              of elements
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e = (n, x)
              is finite. By Proposition
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#countable"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mi n
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E = \sum_n E_n
              is countable, i.e., is enumerated by some bijection
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mo →
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N} \to E
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+section"} global section
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
              of
              = succeed ")" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s(0)
              is the root (the unique element of
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi E
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_0
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s(n) \in F(n)
              has infinitely many descendants, let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s(n+1)
              be the first of its children in the enumeration that also has infinitely many descendants (such children exist since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s(n)
              has only finitely many children by the branching hypothesis).
          %p
            Weak König’s lemma lends its name to one the so-called Big Five systems of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reverse+mathematics"} reverse mathematics
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi WKL
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} WKL_0
          #weak.num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              Actually, the WKL as stated above is stronger than another result that goes by that name, namely that an infinite subtree of the infinite binary tree
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mn 2
                      %mrow
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2^{\lt \omega}
              (consisting of finite 0-1 sequences or words, where the root is the empty word and the parent of a nonempty word is obtained by leaving off the last binary digit) has an infinite branch. This is provable in ZF without choice or other choice-free forms of set theory; the key point is that
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mn 2
                        %mrow
                          %mo &lt;
                          %mi ω
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2^{\lt \omega}
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#countable"}
              to construct one. From there, the recursive construction of a branch in the proof of Corollary
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#wkl"}
              can be performed without making choices.
